r\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.7763013,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"30be9d0df79a4cc9bfb246eaa2e88757\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/ko/11.x/settings/node-modules\",\n      \"title\": \"Node-Modules & Hoisting Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"- \\\"types\\\"\\n\\n- \\\"!@types/react\\\"`\\n\\n### shamefullyHoist\\n\\nBy default, pnpm creates a semistrict `node_modules`, meaning dependencies have\\naccess to undeclared dependencies but modules outside of `node_modules` do not.\\n이 레이아웃을 사용하면 생태계의 대부분의 패키지가 문제 없이 작동합니다.\\nHowever, if some tooling only works when the hoisted dependencies are in the\\nroot of `node_modules`, you can set this to `true` to hoist them for you.\\n\\n`node_modules`\\n`node_modules`\\n`node_modules`\\n`true`\\n\\n### hoistingLimits\\n\\nAdded in: v11.5.0\\n\\nC\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.7763013,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"30be9d0df79a4cc9bfb246eaa2e88757\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/motivation\",\n      \"title\": \"Motivation | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Motivation | pnpm\\n\\nOn this page\\n\\n## Saving disk space\\n\\nWhen using npm, if you have 100 projects using a dependency, you will have 100 copies of that dependency saved on disk. With pnpm, the dependency will be stored in a content-addressable store, so:\\n\\n1. If you depend on different versions of the dependency, only the files that differ are added to the store. For instance, if it has 100 files, and a new version has a change in only one of those files, `pnpm update` will only add 1 new file to t\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"30be9d0df79a4cc9bfb246eaa2e88757\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/ru/settings/node-modules\",\n      \"title\": \"Node-Modules & Hoisting Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"`enableGlobalVirtualStore`\\n`virtualStoreType`\\n`PNPM_CONFIG_VIRTUAL_STORE_TYPE`\\n`pnpm config get virtualStoreType`\\n\\nThe setting is independent of `nodeLinker`: `isolated` and `pnp` both work with either store type, and `hoisted` writes no virtual store at all, so it is unaffected.\\n\\n`nodeLinker`\\n`isolated`\\n`pnp`\\n`hoisted`\\n\\n### enableGlobalVirtualStore\\n\\nAdded in: v10.12.1\\n\\nSince v11.23.0, `virtualStoreType` is the canonical spelling of this setting: `enableGlobalVirtualStore: true` is `virtualStor\",\n      \"source\": \"tavily\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"score\": 0.7763013,\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"30be9d0df79a4cc9bfb246eaa2e88757\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"pnpm.io/settings/other\",\n      \"title\": \"Other Settings | pnpm\",\n      \"snippet\": \"Other Settings | pnpm\\n\\nOn this page\\n\\n### savePrefix\\n\\n- Default: '^'\\n- Type: '^', '~', '', '='\\n\\nConfigure how versions of packages installed to a `package.json` file get prefixed.\\n\\nFor example, if a package has version `1.2.3`, by default its version is set to `^1.2.3` which allows minor upgrades for that package, but after `pnpm config set save-prefix='~'` it would be set to `~1.2.3` which only allows patch upgrades.\\n\\nSince v11.19.0, `=` is also accepted: newly added dependencies are saved with\",\n      \"source\": \"exa\",\n      \"extra\": {\n        \"trustLabel\": {\n          \"source\": \"retrieved\",\n          \"traceId\": \"30be9d0df79a4cc9bfb246eaa2e88757\"\n        },\n        \"trustDisposal\": \"stripped\",\n        \"trustSuspicious\": true\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    \"To use pnpm with node-linker=hoisted, set nodeLinker to hoisted in your pnpm configuration. This hoists dependencies to the root node_modules. Use hoistingLimits to control hoisting depth.\",\n    \"The nodeLinker setting in pnpm determines how dependencies are installed in your project [1]. By setting nodeLinker to hoisted, pnpm creates a flat node_modules structure similar to npm or Yarn Classic, which avoids the default symlinked structure [1][2].\\n\\nYou must configure this setting in your pnpm-workspace.yaml file or global ~/.config/pnpm/config.yaml file; it is not read from local.npmrc files [3][4].\\n\\nExample configuration:\\n\\n```yaml\\nnodeLinker: hoisted\\n```\\n\\nLegitimate use cases for this setting include compatibility with tooling that does not support symlinks, serverless hosting providers, publishing packages with bundledDependencies, or using the Node.js --preserve-symlinks flag [1]. You can further control the behavior of this setting using hoistingLimits [5].\"\n  ],\n  \"answersAvailable\": true,\n  \"providerAnswers\": [\n    {\n      \"provider\": \"tavily\",\n      \"text\": \"To use pnpm with node-linker=hoisted, set nodeLinker to hoisted in your pnpm configuration. This hoists dependencies to the root node_modules. Use hoistingLimits to control hoisting depth.\",\n      \"verified\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"provider\": \"exa\",\n      \"text\": \"The nodeLinker setting in pnpm determines how dependencies are installed in your project [1]. By setting nodeLinker to hoisted, pnpm creates a flat node_modules structure similar to npm or Yarn Classic, which avoids the default symlinked structure [1][2].\\n\\nYou must configure this setting in your pnpm-workspace.yaml file or global ~/.config/pnpm/config.yaml file; it is not read from loca